WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.190
Speaker 1: Let's go next to RJ from Wisconsin.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.910
RJ, you're on the air.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.250
Speaker 3: Hi there, Jan again.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.970
Hey, thank you for taking my call.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:16.850
I want to say I personally am fairly relieved
about the result of yesterday's trial.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:23.170
It seems that upwards of maybe eighty five
to eighty eight percent of America is as well.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:28.500
But I guess I want to touch on something that's
been coming up in the news, and that's the

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.690
shooting that occurred in Columbus as well,
and all of the protests that are coming as

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.019
a result of that.

00:00:36.019 --> 00:00:37.019
Right.

00:00:37.019 --> 00:00:39.640
Watch that video.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.750
Several times, and obviously nobody knows
the exact details of that yet, but I guess

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.440
my question to you is, do we?

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:56.339
Need to in some ways draw a line to where
we're willing to protest some of these things

00:00:56.339 --> 00:00:59.559
if the use of force is.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:07.860
Is is use of force ever a justifiable method
from law enforcement and is there a line that

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:16.250
we are going to draw as Democrats or as as
liberals that.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.690
We would be willing to accept any reason for
use of force by a police officer

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.730
Speaker 1: or so are you are you saying are
there any circumstances where it's OK for

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.370
police to use deadly force?

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.370
Is that your question?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.370
Speaker 3: Yeah, I guess in what circumstance
would that be OK?

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:39.660
I mean, it looked to me from the video and
again, I'm just going off of what I've seen

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.660
in slow motion.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.060
I was but it like the one individual had had
possibly a knife or something like that that

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.530
was going after another individual.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:51.560
Speaker 1: Well, listen, I haven't I haven't
seen this specific video, but certainly there

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.850
are of course, there are situations where
police use of force up to deadly force is

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.490
is warranted.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:06.670
What we want is for as a society to have a
really good feeling that police are good at

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.850
determining when that's appropriate.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.239
And you can go through all sorts of different
scenarios.

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:18.040
And active gunman and police show up certain
hostage situations, someone who's about to

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.239
set off a bomb.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:27.909
I mean, I recognize and accept the legal framework
in which police are able to use up to deadly

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:28.909
force.

00:02:28.909 --> 00:02:33.290
But I think the debate I've not heard any
I mean, OK, I guess there's some people who

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.560
don't want police at all.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.180
And maybe they would say police shouldn't
ever even fire a gun no matter what.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.680
It's more
Speaker 2: far left.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.159
Speaker 1: Ninety nine percent of every 100
percent of everyone I've personally spoken

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:48.890
to understands that sometimes police use deadly
force and it is appropriate.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:56.560
Speaker 3: Right, and I guess I guess my question
is, is, is if there are clear instances where

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.859
obviously police intervention and use of deadly
force is necessary.

00:03:01.859 --> 00:03:02.859
Yes.

00:03:02.859 --> 00:03:06.659
But, you know, given that, you know.

00:03:06.659 --> 00:03:12.201
I guess in terms of going forward with protests
and things like that, if we're, you know,

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:16.799
are we willing to on our side acknowledge
the fact that maybe there is, you know, say

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:20.840
in that situation, if they had, you know,
if the individual did have a knife and was

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.450
going after after the other individual, you
know, are we willing to accept that?

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.419
Yes, because that was an African-American.

00:03:27.419 --> 00:03:33.360
That was that was unfortunately shot by a
white police officer that we're willing to

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.609
admit that that was that was a justifiable
use of force in that instance.

00:03:38.609 --> 00:03:44.250
Speaker 1: Listen, you'd have to ask people
who I guess are on some other side of this.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:49.809
My my view is that just the race of that of
an officer and the race of the person that

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:54.500
gets shot doesn't tell you whether the actions
of the officer were acceptable.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.500
That's my position.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.129
You're getting into a lot of other other things
that don't really reflect my views.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:03.609
So I would maybe talk to people who are more
of that of that mindset.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.959
I appreciate the phone call very much.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.140
And it's an important question, but I think
that it's also important not to.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.859
I mean, police should never use force.

00:04:14.859 --> 00:04:18.010
Period is not one I've really heard.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:22.510
So I'm not inclined to spend too much time
arguing about it because it seems ninety nine

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.780
point nine percent of the country is on the
same page on that.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.780
OK, I'm up.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:27.780
I apologize.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.200
I wasn't able to pick up a more phone calls.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:31.480
I did what I could.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.380
We will take a break and we will take calls
again.

